<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ministeriales</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ministerialis, Ministerialen, Dienstmann, Dienstmänner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ministeriales were a legally unfree but socially elite class of knights, administrators and officials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, serving secular and church lords. Recruited at first from household serfs, they managed estates, finances and castles and held the household offices of seneschal, butler, marshal and chamberlain; Emperor Conrad II (990–1039) first referred to them as a distinct class. Their lords decided whom they might marry, and children of mixed marriages usually took the status of the lesser parent. In the 13th century their status was assimilated to that of the free nobility, and in the 15th century they formed the core of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> class.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the German lands most knights were <b>ministeriales</b>, legally unfree servants of the emperor, princes and bishops, who nevertheless ranked above free commoners and by the 13th century had merged with the lesser nobility.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>The lordship took its name from the Schellenberg family, Bavarian <b>ministeriales</b> who settled in the Rhine valley in the 13th century and built castles on the Eschnerberg ridge.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-schellenberg">Lordship of Schellenberg</a></li></ul></section>
